# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROD environment — Arbitrum One (chainId 42161), real Chainlink Data Streams
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env.prod
#
# This deploys against REAL USDT with REAL user funds. The live prod Settlement
# held 557.24 USDT across 14,267 markets as of 2026-08-10 and was still trading
# while these values were read. Treat every step as a live-money operation.
#
# Every non-secret value below was read off the live prod contracts and is the
# exact config the current deployment runs with. Deploying from this file
# reproduces prod like-for-like: the deployed bytecode is byte-identical to this
# branch (audit/hacken-remediation-v2 @ 274331d) after masking immutables +
# metadata.
#
# BEFORE YOU DEPLOY, read the two blockers at the bottom of this file. The
# Chainlink allow-list one has external lead time and will silently break
# resolution if skipped.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Secrets — GENERATE FRESH, never reuse the previous developer's keys ──────
# 0x-prefixed hex. Deploy.s.sol reads this with vm.envUint, so the 0x is
# required. This key becomes the initial owner of all three contracts; hand
# ownership to the Safe afterwards (see OWNER_ADDRESS below).
# Fund it with ETH on Arbitrum One before deploying.
DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x

ARBITRUM_RPC_URL=
ARBISCAN_API_KEY=

# ── Roles — GENERATE FRESH ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Relayer: hot key held by the backend; submits enterPosition / mint / redeemFor.
# This is the key most exposed to the previous developer — rotate it first and
# make sure the backend is redeployed with the new one before cutover.
RELAYER_ADDRESS=0x
# Treasury: receives platformFee and funds rebate claims. Must be non-zero —
# any fill with platformFee > 0 reverts TreasuryNotConfigured.
# Confirm who controls the outgoing treasury EOA before deciding whether this
# needs to change; it may belong to the client rather than the old developer.
TREASURY_ADDRESS=0x

# ── Collateral + feeds (live prod values, carry over unchanged) ──────────────
# Real USDT on Arbitrum One.
USDT_ADDRESS=0xFd086bC7CD5C481DCC9C85ebE478A1C0b69FCbb9

# REAL Chainlink Data Streams VerifierProxy on Arbitrum One. Read off the live
# prod resolver — this is the genuine one, not a mock.
CHAINLINK_VERIFIER_PROXY_ADDRESS=0x478Aa2aC9F6D65F84e09D9185d126c3a17c2a93C

CHAINLINK_LINK_TOKEN_ADDRESS=0xf97f4df75117a78c1A5a0DBb814Af92458539FB4
CHAINLINK_BTC_USD_FEED=0x6ce185860a4963106506C203335A2910413708e9
CHAINLINK_ETH_USD_FEED=0x639Fe6ab55C921f74e7fac1ee960C0B6293ba612
CHAINLINK_SEQUENCER_FEED=0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D

# ── Migration mode — LEAVE UNSET ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Setting this preserves the OLD Settlement, which is the opposite of a key
# rotation: the previous owner keeps full control of it, including the ability
# to drain collateral via proposeEmergencyWithdraw. Fresh deploy only.
# EXISTING_SETTLEMENT_ADDRESS=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POST-DEPLOY — not read by Deploy.s.sol; applied by hand afterwards
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# REAL Chainlink stream IDs currently configured on the live prod resolver
# (the 0x0003… prefix marks them as genuine DON stream IDs). Re-apply to the
# new resolver:
#   cast send $RESOLVER "configureStreamsFeed(bytes32,bytes32)" $(cast keccak "BTC/USD") $STREAMS_FEED_ID_BTC_USD
#   cast send $RESOLVER "configureStreamsFeed(bytes32,bytes32)" $(cast keccak "ETH/USD") $STREAMS_FEED_ID_ETH_USD
STREAMS_FEED_ID_BTC_USD=0x00039d9e45394f473ab1f050a1b963e6b05351e52d71e507509ada0c95ed75b8
STREAMS_FEED_ID_ETH_USD=0x000362205e10b3a147d02792eccee483dca6c7b44ecce7012cb8c6e0b68b3ae9

# Prod runs the keeper from a SEPARATE EOA, not the relayer. Deploy.s.sol sets
# cycler.forwarder = RELAYER_ADDRESS, so this is a REQUIRED post-deploy fixup:
#   cast send $CYCLER "setForwarder(address)" $KEEPER_FORWARDER_ADDRESS
# Generate fresh. (The outgoing forwarder is a plain EOA, not a Chainlink
# Automation forwarder contract — prod is on the cron stopgap, not Automation.)
KEEPER_FORWARDER_ADDRESS=0x

# Final owner. Deploy.s.sol transfers to this as its last broadcast step when
# set. All three contracts are Ownable2Step, so this only sets pendingOwner and
# the target must call acceptOwnership() to complete it — a wrong address here
# cannot lock you out. Use a Safe multisig: owner can rewire the resolver and
# drain collateral, so a single hot key is what you are trying to get away from.
# There is deliberately no OWNER_PRIVATE_KEY on prod —
# `npm run accept-ownership:prod` prints the three addresses for the Safe UI
# rather than signing anything locally.
OWNER_ADDRESS=0x

# ── Ops targets (npm run …) — fill in AFTER the deploy ──────────────────────
# Copy these from the deployments/prod-<block>.json record the deploy writes.
SETTLEMENT_ADDRESS=
RESOLVER_ADDRESS=
CYCLER_ADDRESS=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUPERSEDED — not part of the deploy
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ThinWalletFactory was never deployed to prod and is not needed. The Settlement
# verifies signatures with OpenZeppelin SignatureChecker, which accepts any
# ERC-1271 account, and the popup-less order-session flow shipped on rain.trade's
# Alchemy Modular Account v2 (see test/OrderSessionForkTest.t.sol).

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BLOCKERS — resolve these before the deploy, not after
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. CHAINLINK ALLOW-LIST. The current prod resolver address is authorized with
#    the DON for the two stream IDs above. A freshly deployed resolver is NOT.
#    verify() will fail until Chainlink allow-lists the new address, which means
#    no resolution. Open that request BEFORE deploying — it is external lead time
#    you cannot compress on the day.
#
# 2. LINK FUNDING. Both the dev and prod resolvers currently hold ZERO LINK,
#    despite prod being on the real DON. ChainlinkResolver holds its own LINK and
#    pays per-verify from that balance (Option A funding, ChainlinkResolver.sol:31).
#    Either the FeeManager is returning a zero fee for your account, or Streams
#    verification is not actually on the live resolution path. Find out which
#    before cutover — the new resolver inherits whatever the real answer is. If
#    fees are non-zero, budget the 5–10 LINK floor cited in Deploy.s.sol:140 and
#    top up by direct transfer:  LINK.transfer(resolver, amount)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OUTGOING DEPLOYMENT — for the cutover/drain, not for Deploy.s.sol
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LIVE with real user collateral. The previous owner retains full control of
# these contracts forever; redeploying does not change that. They can
# proposeEmergencyWithdraw and, after the 24h EMERGENCY_TIMELOCK, take the USDT —
# or setResolver to decide the outcome of every open market. Quiesce, resolve
# open markets, and get user funds out BEFORE the new stack goes live.
#   OLD_SETTLEMENT=0x7978871Ebd82511d2f27da02507AD97b65f734e3   (557.24 USDT, 14,267 markets)
#   OLD_RESOLVER=0xE1D74Ea6d3024dc52e66B5707Eb6D00BABf08301
#   OLD_CYCLER=0x9872bb8197b3052d0c9fe1a57e83b19f9319787c
#   OLD_OWNER=0xfeA2965fa9c2862B6F23E5EA6D94B34E38B82e01
#   OLD_TREASURY=0x897292882a2811B19D213CDe85e0E228E51E6c9f
#   OLD_RELAYER=0xF552155d3e6E33CEc53Ce30cC171D78207825018
#   OLD_FORWARDER=0xD1424ab5e04C5c305324042bc6D79002901830ed
